# OxizeOS Changelog

## `<Date> <Version>: <Title>`
- Version is in format "`<Edition>`.`<Major>`.`<Minor>`.`<Build>`
- Build number is forever incremental with each update, never resets

## 2026-01-07 1.0.0.0001: Initial Update

## 2026-02-22 1.0.1.0002: Minimal bootloader

- Implemented a minimal UEFI Bootloader (BootMgr.exe) that does not support other boot protocols, such as multiboot2
- Implemented an example task scheduler (tskschl.exe) that for now clears the UEFI GOP Framebuffer
- Introduced a new dependency for scripts: python 3

## 2026-02-27 1.0.1.0003: Paging Driver Update

- Fixed a problem where the `image.json` contained an entry to copy a non-existing file
- Fixed a bug where gen_isrs.py was adding `#pragma once` to gen_isrs.cpp
- Refractored some code in Boot Manager
- Finished implementing the paging driver in the Task Scheduler
- Introduced a new method for managing page tables: Free List
- Added global constructor management trough the use of .init_array section in the task scheduler entry point
- Fixed a bug where the toolchain-gcc.mk was making GCC build without it generating .init_array section in ELF files

## 2026-03-06 1.0.1.0004: PCIe Driver Implementation

- Fixed an inefficiency where the task scheduler was being generated with debug symbols, inflating the executable size and compile times
- Made the target `toolchain` inside `srcipts/toolchain-gcc.mk` no longer compile the i686 targets by default, because currently they're not supported in the OS and not used
- Implemented an ACPI 2.0+ Driver that currently only parses the XSDT for tables, and currently only returns the MCFG Table
- Implemented an extensive PCIe Driver with support for all 256 busses, multi-function devices, BAR handling, PCI-PCI Bridge handling, etc.
- Cleaned up, `main.cpp`, added code to test the PCIe driver by finding a SATA IntelAHCI device and printing out it's data
- Updated the `run` script by extending the qemu script to expose the disk as a SATA IntelAHCI device instead of the legacy ATA(IDE) device

## 2026-03-29 1.0.1.0005: SATA IntelAHCI Driver Implementation

- Implemented a minimal **SATA IntelAHCI** Driver for reading from the disk, not much testing was done, but should work on **qemu**
- Did some minor bugfixes to various parts of code to fix compiler warnings
- Minor updates to README and CHANGELOG

## 2026-03-29 1.0.1.0006: Build System Refractor

- Added multi-thread compilation to speed up compile times
- Added a `build` shell script to avoid calling `make -j$(nproc) -s` directly
- Cleaned up the Makefile
- Added sparse image support by using qemu-img instead of dd
- Added a new tool for formatting **GPT** disks to replace gdisk which was taking a lot of time
- Updated the `Image` tool to use `qemu-img` instead of `dd` and add support for various disk formats supported by `qemu-img` and
updated it to use the new **GPT** formatting tool instead of gdisk
- Refractored build system informative output to be more color coded, easier to parse and not use repetitive information
- Made the compilation times ~3-5x faster than before

## 2026-04-10 1.0.1.0007: small general refractoring

- Added a new `resources` folder that contains constant resources copied to the image
- Added new `VOLID.CONF` files(although on host they're named differently) containing some volume information that normally cannot be stored inside FS internals
- Updated image.json to format the OS partition, add some directories and copy a test file. For now the main OS partition will use FAT32 until I develop a new file system
- Removed redundant `MdeModulePkg`, `ShellPkg` and `CryptoPkg` include folders in `lib/UEFI/` that were taking up space

## 2026-04-13 1.0.1.0008 Image tool reimplementation

- Redesigned the image configuration file from JSON to a custom one. documentation can be found in `resources/docs/image.md`
- Reimplemented the entire `Image` tool, removing the old one written in rust and parsing JSON to one written in C++ that uses a custom configuration format

## 2026-04-17 1.0.1.0009 FAT32 Driver refinement

- Added better label support by making MKFS also add a VOLUME_ID entry to the root directory
- Added support for timestamps, where previously they were skipped, and now added and updated wherever needed

## 2026-04-19 1.0.1.0010 Kernel and IRQ implementation

- Changed the task scheduler to be a part of the kernel instead of being a standalone executable that runs before the kernel(design only, task scheduler not yet implemented)
- Renamed the task scheduler to **SysKrnl64**
- Removed the i686 toolchain build entries from `scripts/toolchain-gcc.mk`, as I don't plan to use or integrate it
- Added a vector class and some extra utilities from STD namespace to SysKrnl64
- Added an **APIC** driver and capable of handling **X2APIC** & multiple IOAPIC devices
- Added an **IRQ** dispatcher class and a IntHandler class where **IRQ** handlers will be placed
- Updated the `run` script, specifically the **qemu** part to use the new OVMF_CODE.fd & OVMF_VARS.fd binary files

## 2026-06-17 1.0.1.0011 Kernel Refractor and Memory Management

- Added a physical memory allocator, which is a bitmap allocator for allocating physical memory in 4KiB blocks
- Added a virtual memory allocator, which is a Red-Black tree range allocator that stores ranges describing the allocated / reserved regions, works in 4KiB blocks
- Added a kernel heap allocator, which is similar to a linked-list allocator, but works in byte granularity and has headers before allocated memory to describe it's flags, length and the neighboring headers
- Split the **SysKrnl64** `main()` function into `kernel_bootstrap()` and `kernel_main()` functions, where `kernel_bootstrap()` sets up the paging driver, interrupts, MSRs and the memory drivers, and then allocates a new stack(512KiB), and returns the pointer to the end of it, then the assembly entry point function updates RSP to the new stack and calls `kernel_main()` with the new stack
- Removed the assembly `memset` implementation which was buggy, and swapped builtin `memcmp` and `memcpy` implementations to manual C++ implementations, with `memset` and `memcpy` using quad words for better speed
- Added `kmalloc`, `kcalloc` and `kfree` functions to `stdio.hpp` for general purpose heap memory allocation
- Fixed some bugs inside the AHCI driver that caused the byte count in PRDT entry be incorrect(off by 1) and not setting `fis->c` to 1
- Fixed a bug in `vprintf` where %p was treated like a regular hex number, instead of a 64-bit address specifier
- Removed the hardcoded 64MiB kernel data area, instead the virtual allocator will be used for kernel memory allocations
- Made the bootstrap kernel stack from 512KiB to 32KiB, the kernel later allocates it's own with the virtual allocator
- Added and integrated `std::expected`(C++23) to the kernel for better error handling
- Refractored kernel headers to have descriptors about where the header is located and it's purpose
- Added some extra comment refractoring to standard libraries

## 2026-07-22 1.0.1.0012 Hardware Timers and Current Time

- Changed the project license to **GPLv3**
- Implemented drivers for the **HPET**, **LAPIC Timer**, **Invariant TSC**. **LAPIC timer** currently untested, **HPET** and **iTSC** are working
- Implemented a **Timer** class that provides functions to pause the current LP for a specfied amount of time and also get the SystemTime struct describing the current time since a custom epoch, which is **January 1st, 2000th year, UTC 00:00:00**
- Implemented a **Time** class that abstracts the **SystemTime** from the **Timer** class and provides human readable time data
- Reorganized the **ACPI** driver to remove code duplications
- Added an extra attribute to printf that makes gcc / clang compilers emit warnings if format mismatchs are detected(for example: `printf("%d\r\n", "test")` would emit a warning, because `%d` expects an `int`, but `"test"` is a string)
- Renamed folder `lib/UEFI` to `lib/EDK2`
- Removed the `libcppextra` library, which was not used and I didn't plan to use it in the future
- Added a `const_array.hpp` file to the **SysKrnl64** which is similar to std::vector, but cannot be resized dynamically, but the count can be non-static and data lives on the heap
- Made the UEFI Bootloader call `ExitBootServices()` before switching `CR3` to ensure a clean UEFI boot services shutdown
- Updated the APIC driver to allow assigning GSIs to hardware and specifying trigger and polarity types
- Added the `uint128_t` and `int128_t` types to **SysKrnl64** `stdint.hpp` which use the gcc `__uint128_t` and `__int128_t` types
- Moved `kmalloc`, `kcalloc` and `kfree` in SysKrnl64 from `stdio.hpp` to `stdlib.hpp` to match the standard conventions
- Updated the **APIC** driver to mark the **GSIs** that MADT ISO entries describe as having an IRQ remapped to them, used
- Updated the toolchain to use binutils 2.46.1
- Fixed a bug in the SysKrnl64 vector class that could cause double-free when using copy ctors/assignments
- Updated the `run` script to run with linux **KVM** and host CPU emulation for more features and faster emulation
- Noticed the `FAT32` tool is buggier than expected, and it's design is starting to cause problems, next update should handle those problems

## 2026-08-01 1.0.1.0013 FAT32 Tool Rewrite

- Removed the old FAT32 tool and implemented a new one
- This new tool has a lot less operations, but they're enough for the image building process, and other operations the previous tool had were unused and taking up program space
- Made the partition manager in the new tool support both MBR and GPT, support MBR logical partitions, do extensive verifications and a minimal recovery interface
- Changed the tool input argument structure
- Added better support for wide strings, supporting wchar_t being both 2 and 4 bytes long
- Replaced the mkfs operation with mkfs.fat, as it's a lot more trusted
- Added FAT12 and FAT16 support to the FAT Driver

## 2026-08-16 1.0.1.0014 Switch from ELF to PE in the kernel

- Changed the kernel executable format from **ELF** to **PE**
- The change was made because PE offers features such as:
- - Built-in resource script compilers and resource sections, which ELF doesn't provide out of the box(would need to implement custom tools)
- - Removes the cross-platform gcc & binutils toolchain dependency, as for PE the host MinGW is fully usable
- - And some other reasons, like the way PE handles DLLs or relocations

- Removed forced `Global.hpp` include from both the boot manager and the kernel, mainly because dependency tracking with those forced includes are nearly useless
- Added dependency tracking to the boot manager and the kernel, so if a header is changed, all source files that include that header would be recompiled
- Updated most kernel assembly source files to place the code in a special `.code` section to separate assembly and C++ code and also to use the Microsoft x86-64 ABI
- Added a `syskrnl64.rc` resource script at the root of the **SysKrnl64** subproject folder for managing PE Resources used by the kernel
- Made the kernel assembly entry point put the code in a special `.entry` section to separate the entry point code from regular code
- Added dependency tracking to the kernel and the bootloader so when a header file is changed, all source files that include this header would be recompiled
- Did some refractoring on the kernel linker script and makefile
- Changed the `isr.asm` in the kernel to use a much more safer stack frame construction and dispatching mechanizm
- Added a `readhex` operation to the FAT32 Driver debug cmd operation that reads files and returns formatted hex dumps of their data
- Added a `std::string` class for easier string operations
- Added a `debug` flag to the `./run qemu` operation to launch qemu paused, with a port for GDB to attach to, and also CPU Exception logging to stdio with kvm disabled
- Refractored the kernel file layout, making it more architecture aware, and separating drivers from APIs
- Changed the namespace structure from SysKrnl64::`<Driver name>` to krnl::`<Driver name>`_`<object name>`
- Some other refractoring

## 2026-09-05 1.0.1.0015 Base of Multi-Processing support

- Added standard libraries:
- - `<mutex>`: serializes access to a function so it would only be executed by one LP at a time
- - `<atomic>`: atomically manages access to a variable / object between LPs
- - `<deque>`: a double-ended queue implementation, using a simpler algorithm to provide the needed functionality for `std::queue`
- - `<queue>`: a FIFO style queue for queueing events to be executed by something

- Added APIs:
- `HandleMgr`: a handle manager allowing to store various objects in handles with a safe accessing mechanizm

- Added Drivers:
- `LCPU`: A driver for managing initialization and event queueing to other LPs

- Added Utilities:
- `<alloc.hpp>`: Containts an utility function for allocating stacks

- Changes:
- Serialized some drivers that might be used by multiple LPs, such as the Memory Management drivers, handle manager, the LP event queue and the `printf` function
- Made the BSP also enter the event handler loop, like the other LPs, to implement SMP correctly
- Made all LPs fully initialize themselves to 64-bit long mode with the needed drivers set up(GDT, IDT, TSS, LPData, etc.)
- Fixed a bug with the PE Loader in the boot loader where it was doing incorrect verifications on sections not meant to be loaded to memory
- Made the `KRNL_STATUS` enum become the centralized error code list, instead of having separate ones, for example, `API_STATUS` or `MemoryAllocErrors`